<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nagoya</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nagoya is the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aichi-prefecture">Aichi Prefecture</a> and one of the largest cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>. It grew from the castle built from 1610 on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a>'s orders, to which the town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kiyosu">Kiyosu</a> was moved, and served as the seat of the Owari Tokugawa, the senior <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gosanke">Gosanke</a> house. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-muneharu">Tokugawa Muneharu</a> in the 1730s it became known for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> and commerce, and it is now a centre of the car and aerospace industries.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nagoya.jpg" alt="Nagoya" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: ウィキ太郎(WikiTaro) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nagoya_Station_-_View_from_the_Main_Building_in_Nagoya_Campus_of_Aichi_University_2022-6-29.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
